A||||16||loafer87gt||What sort of speeds are you DSL users getting?||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||01-12-2001||08:18 PM||||i swicthed last year from cable to DSL and I have been very dissapointed with the overall performance. I used to do quite a bit of online gaming, and I can no longer do that as my pings are 250+ and I have severe packetloss. I recently went to <A HREF="http://www.dslreports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dslreports.com</A>  to check my download/upload speeds, and was dissapointed to find that I was only getting 190 download, 53 upload,a bout 3x faster than a 56 K modem. The two guys upstairs did the same test and they scored 1100 kbps download, and 123 upload. We are using the same hardware, and the only difference is that we are using different phone lines. I called Sasktel Sympatico (my provider) and they said my line is OK, and that they only guarantee that you are able to get connected to the internet, not the advertised 1500kbps that made me switch from Shaw@home. I was wondering if some of you guys would take the speed test at the <A HREF="http://www.dslreports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dslreports.com</A>  page and let me know what your speeds are. It can be found under tools on the left hand part of the page, thanks. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||baldlobo||01-12-2001||08:42 PM||||heres what I got(@home Cable link) 394 down/ 451up kbps||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||01-12-2001||09:09 PM||||I tried it at 9:00 pm est and got 727 dwn and 277 up whever that means.  @home.com (cable)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 01-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jimmy||01-12-2001||09:33 PM||||Run the tweaks at dslreports.com. They fixed my initial slow speeds.<P>Jim ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||01-12-2001||09:49 PM||||Here's mine...<P>** Speed 623(down)/127(up) kbps **<BR>(At least 12 times faster than a 56k modem)<P>@Home in Nashville caps our output. There is nothing we can do about that.||||reg||1
Z||000005||LarryB||01-13-2001||12:54 AM||||1304/86 using AT&T Broadband (former MediaOne)<P>If you're running Windows you can try using the tracert command to see where the bottleneck is, or you can use a looking glass to see the route back to you.  Go to <A HREF="http://nitrous.digex.net" TARGET=_blank>http://nitrous.digex.net</A>  and you can get traces back from several of the US public NAPs (Washington DC, New Jersey,  San Jose/San Fransisco area). Your output will look something like this:<P>Type escape sequence to abort.<BR>Tracing the route to 209.244.125.7<P>  1 phl2-core2-fa0-1-0.atlas.icix.net(165.117.51.37) 4 msec 4 msec 0 msec<BR>  2 dca1-core12-s3-2.atlas.icix.net (165.117.51.33) 8 msec<BR>    dca1-core12-s3-3.atlas.icix.net (165.117.52.121) 8 msec<BR>    dca1-core12-s3-2.atlas.icix.net (165.117.51.33) 8 msec<BR>  3 serial3-0-4.core1.washington1.level3.net (209.244.219.161) 8 msec 8 msec 4 msec<BR>  4 so-4-0-0.mp1.Washington1.level3.net (209.247.10.65) [AS 3356] 8 msec 8 msec 8 msec<BR>  5 loopback0.hsipaccess2.Boston1.Level3.net (209.244.3.33) [AS 3356] 20 msec 20 msec 20 msec<BR>  6 209.244.125.7 [AS 3356] 20 msec 16 msec 20 msec<P><BR>(that final address is one of my machines)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||loafer87gt||01-13-2001||12:55 AM||||I tried the using the registry tweaks and all that seemed to do is increase my packet loss. I am going to take my computer over and it hook it up to my landlords phone line and see if my speeds increase to around 1000 like they are getting. If they do, it should serve as proof to the phone company that my line is knackered.||||reg||1
